 **Edge Cases and Pitfalls:**
-*   **Fidelity Loss:** It is not possible in general to quantify the reconstruction error introduced by the network [S1]. The network learns general geometric properties of isocontours rather than specific isosurfaces, so it works on unseen data, but precise error metrics are hard to define [S1].
-*   **Color Bleeding:** Training on color images directly leads to color bleeding and shifts [S1].
-*   **Temporal Coherence:** Without temporal loss during training, frame-to-frame coherence may be poor for interactive navigation [S1].
+*   The network does not learn specific isosurfaces but general geometric properties of isocontours, allowing it to work on unseen isosurfaces [S1].
+*   It is not possible in general to quantify the reconstruction error introduced by the network, making fidelity loss hard to measure precisely [S1].
 
 ## Operating characteristics
-**Time and Memory Complexity:**
-*   **Time:** The method achieves a performance gain of one to two orders of magnitude compared to full-resolution ray-casting [S1].
-    *   Example (Source [S1]): Ground truth render at $1280 \times 960$ takes 0.16 sec (no AO) and 18.6 sec (with AO). Super-resolution takes 0.07 sec.
-    *   The speedup factor is $16 \times N_{AO}$ when AO is included, where $N_{AO}$ is the number of AO samples per point [S1].
-*   **Memory:** Requires memory for the low-resolution depth/normal buffers and the high-resolution output buffers. The neural network model itself requires GPU memory, but this is constant and independent of resolution [S1].
-
-**Measured Performance:**
-*   Hardware/Resolution: Benchmarks in [S1] used a $1024^3$ volumetric grid. Input: $320 \times 240$. Output: $1280 \times 960$.
-*   Speedup: 0.07 sec for SR vs 0.16 sec (no AO) and 18.6 sec (with AO) for ground truth [S1].
-
-**Strengths and Limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Drastic reduction in volumetric samples (factor of 16 without AO). Eliminates runtime AO simulation. Works on isosurfaces not seen during training [S1]. Good for interactive navigation with temporal coherence [S1].
-*   **Limitations:** Reconstruction error is hard to quantify [S1]. Relies on a trained neural network, which may fail on data distributions significantly different from training (though generalization to unseen isosurfaces is reported) [S1]. Not suitable for applications requiring exact geometric fidelity where error bounds are critical [S1].
-
-**When to Use:**
-*   Remote visualization, in-situ visualization, and focus+context visualization for complex isosurfaces [S1].
-*   Interactive exploration where frame rate is more important than absolute geometric precision [S1].
-
-**When Not to Use:**
-*   Applications requiring precise, quantifiable geometric accuracy [S1].
-*   Scenes with isosurfaces that have geometric properties fundamentally different from the training set (though the source claims generalization, this is a risk) [S1].
+*   **Time Complexity:** The method achieves a performance gain of one to two orders of magnitude compared to full-resolution ray-casting [S1].
+*   **Memory Complexity:** Not given in the sources.
+*   **Measured Performance:** In the paper's example (Fig. 1), ground truth render time is 0.16 seconds (without AO) and 18.6 seconds (with AO) at $1280 \times 960$ resolution. The super-resolution step takes 0.07 seconds [S1].
+*   **Scaling:** The method reduces samples by a factor of 16 without AO, and $16 \times N_{AO}$ with AO [S1].
+*   **Strengths:** Avoids runtime AO simulation; works on unseen isosurfaces; maintains temporal coherence with motion loss [S1].
+*   **Limitations:** Reconstruction error is hard to quantify; relies on the quality of the trained network [S1].
+*   **When to use:** Remote visualization, in-situ visualization, and focus+context visualization for complex isosurfaces [S1].
 
 ## Background
-**Origin:**
-*   Introduced by Sebastian Weiss, Mengyu Chu, Nils Thuerey, and Rüdiger Westermann from the Technical University of Munich [S1].
-*   Published on arXiv on June 15, 2019 (arXiv:1906.06520v1) [S1].
-*   Based on the FRVSRNet architecture for video super-resolution [S1].
-
-**History and Development:**
-*   The method addresses the core problem of reducing data samples in isosurface ray-casting [S1]. It builds on recent developments in image and video super-resolution via artificial neural networks [S1].
-*   It distinguishes itself from traditional SR by using 3D scene information (depth and normal fields) instead of color images, allowing it to learn geometric relations and infer AO [S1].
-
-**Usage:**
-*   Potential applications include remote visualization, in-situ visualization, and focus+context visualization for complex isosurfaces [S1].
-
-## Subtopics
+The method was introduced by Sebastian Weiss, Mengyu Chu, Nils Thuerey, and Rüdiger Westermann from the Technical University of Munich, published on arXiv on June 15, 2019 (arXiv:1906.06520v1) [S1]. It builds on recent developments in image and video super-resolution via artificial neural networks to further reduce the number of data access operations in volumetric ray-casting [S1].
 
